WEBVTT

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.455
Validation is where threat hunting becomes disciplined analysis.

00:00:03.455 --> 00:00:07.435
Not every unusual event is suspicious, not every suspicious event is malicious,

00:00:07.435 --> 00:00:09.770
and not every malicious-looking event is confirmed.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:13.610
These words should mean different things in notes and reports. Unusual means the activity

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.735
differs from a baseline or expectation.

00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:19.500
Suspicious means the activity has characteristics that could align with adversary

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.245
behavior.

00:00:20.245 --> 00:00:23.940
Malicious means there is evidence of harmful or unauthorized activity.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.920
Confirmed means the evidence has reached the threshold your organization uses for incident

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.618
handling. A validation workflow starts with context.

00:00:30.618 --> 00:00:34.430
Compare the event against baselines. Has this user authenticated from this location

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:37.135
before? Does this server normally run administrative scripts?

00:00:37.135 --> 00:00:40.940
Is the process common for that software package? Is the destination domain part of a

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:41.865
vendor service?

00:00:41.865 --> 00:00:45.940
Baselines are not perfect, but they help identify what requires deeper review. Next,

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.995
check asset role and identity context.

00:00:47.995 --> 00:00:52.120
A command that is normal on an administrator jump host may be unusual on a finance

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:52.880
workstation.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.960
A service account authenticating at night may be expected if it runs a scheduled job,

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.680
but suspicious if it appears on endpoints it never touches.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.020
A developer workstation may generate traffic that would be odd for a point-of-sale system.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:06.980
Context prevents flat rules from becoming false positives.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.240
Review related events.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:12.290
A single process event may look odd, but the parent process, command line, user,

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:13.860
network connection, file write,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.755
and surrounding authentication events can change the interpretation.

00:01:16.755 --> 00:01:21.090
If a DNS request is unusual, identify the clients, timing, proxy activity,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.570
and endpoint process context when available.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.840
If a privileged logon is unusual, review the source, target, authentication type,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.120
and nearby administrative actions. Look for business explanations.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.425
Maintenance windows, software deployment tools, vulnerability scanners, backup jobs,

00:01:35.425 --> 00:01:38.420
and help desk activity can all produce strange patterns.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.560
Validating benign explanations does not weaken the hunt. It improves the conclusion and

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.300
may produce useful allow lists or tuning guidance.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.185
Preserve evidence before escalation.

00:01:47.185 --> 00:01:52.110
Save relevant event identifiers, query outputs, timestamps, affected assets, user context,

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:54.770
screenshots if appropriate, and the queries used.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.790
Do not alter systems or collect data beyond your authorization.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:02.160
If evidence suggests active compromise, credential misuse, unauthorized access,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.175
or material risk, escalate to incident response through the defined process.

00:02:06.175 --> 00:02:09.425
Careful language matters. Say what the evidence shows, what it suggests,

00:02:09.425 --> 00:02:10.660
and what remains unknown.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:14.480
That discipline protects the credibility of the hunt and helps responders prioritize the

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.220
right work.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.920
False positives are not failures when they teach the team something.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.530
A false positive can reveal a legitimate administrative workflow, a missing asset tag,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.180
a noisy service account, or a detection that needs enrichment.

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:29.110
Record that learning. The next hunt or alert rule can use the context to reduce noise

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.540
without losing visibility.
